{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01PQBczW8hj6EEGfeEZjF1qt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to generate detailed predicted experimental results for all \"planned\" experiments in the experiment_plan, under the \"worst\" tier scenario. Let me carefully review:\n\n**Worst tier definition:**\n- Real contamination negligible or too heterogeneous to model\n- Ranking improvement non-significant\n- Confounding effect near zero in practice\n- Pivot to diagnostic/methodological tool paper (Tier B+)\n\n**Key metric ranges for worst:**\n- real_cn_median: [0.05, 0.3]\n- kendall_tau_gmfrm: [0.74, 0.8]\n\n\n- kendall_tau_best_baseline: [0.74, 0.81]\n- delta_tau_vs_best_baseline: [-0.02, 0.01]\n- delta_tau_p_value: [0.1, 0.8]\n- theta_rmse_reduction_vs_sequential: [-0.05, 0.05]\n- confounding_scatter_r: [0.0, 0.25]\n- convergence_rhat_max: [1.05, 1.2]\n- convergence_ess_min: [30, 100]\n- stat_validation_corr: [0.15, 0.45]\n- ei_pipeline_internal_consistency: [0.1, 0.35]\n\nI'm working with the assumption that the true confounding distribution is either heavily skewed below 0.3 or bimodal, with weak signals coming through the exposure pipeline and minimal ranking improvements. The convergence diagnostics suggest some instability across benchmarks, and the post-hoc power analysis indicates the study is underpowered. I'm planning a series of experiments to address these gaps: collecting multi-judge data, refining item exposure estimation, fitting the model on real data, comparing against baselines, and calibrating contamination magnitude.\n\nNow I'm reviewing the full experiment pipeline and noticing some discrepancies between the planned status and what's actually running—exp-001 and exp-009 are marked as running in the registry despite showing as planned in the experiment plan. I need to reconcile these statuses before proceeding with the remaining experiments like judge sensitivity analysis, exposure ablation, facet diagnostics, and the wildbench replication. I need to cover all experiments marked as \"planned\" in the experiment_plan, regardless of their current status in the registry. So I'll include exp-009 even though it's running, but I can skip exp-012 since it's already complete with actual results. Let me work through the worst-case scenarios for each planned experiment: exp-001 involves data collection where the worst case means judges show limited severity variance and models reveal minimal contamination signals; exp-002 focuses on item exposure estimation where worst-case performance means the pipeline produces weak noisy signals with low internal consistency and minimal performance gaps between variants.\n\nFor exp-003, the worst scenario involves convergence problems with high R̂ values and low effective sample sizes, causing c_n estimates to cluster near zero with negligible differences from sequential approaches. In exp-004, G-MFRM fails to significantly outperform baselines with near-zero or slightly negative Δτ and high p-values, potentially losing to competing methods like CyclicJudge or UDA. For exp-005, the real contamination magnitudes fall well below assumptions with most models near zero and only a few outliers. I'm now looking at exp-006 to understand the worst-case sensitivity to judge count.\n\nJudge count provides minimal benefit when the contamination signal is already weak—even increasing to eight judges doesn't yield meaningful improvements. Moving through the ablation studies, the exposure indicators all show weak effects with no single source dominating, and leave-one-out analysis reveals minimal changes. The facet fit diagnostics reveal poor parameter estimates with high infit/outfit values, divergent transitions, and posterior predictive checks indicating over-dispersion. The formal confounding observation holds theoretically, but Monte Carlo simulations under realistic parameters show the effect is actually small, and synthetic validation demonstrates poor recovery when true contamination is small. The WildBench replication mirrors the MT-Bench and Arena-Hard patterns with no significant improvement, and G-MFRM shows fragility under adversarial conditions.\n\nNow I'm formatting this as YAML with detailed metrics, rationales, and supporting tables.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14170,"cache_read_input_tokens":11447,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14170},"output_tokens":27,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"025abe8b-2d2c-421e-a4ec-955a56bb312b","uuid":"1fd27908-cbd9-489e-8737-89c4940d928b"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_015gHqfM6mkxUtLymn7YcrSa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to generate detailed predicted results for each \"planned\" experiment in the experiment_plan, under the \"worst\" tier scenario. Let me carefully go through the worst tier definition and each experiment to produce realistic predictions.\n\nThe worst tier means:\n- Real contamination negligible or too heterogeneous to model\n- Ranking improvement non-significant\n- Confounding effect near zero in practice\n- Pivot to diagnostic/methodological tool paper (Tier B+)\n\nKey metric ranges for worst:\n- real_cn_median: [0.05, 0.3]\n- kendall_tau_gmfrm: [0.74, 0.8]\n\n\n- kendall_tau_best_baseline: [0.74, 0.81]\n- delta_tau_vs_best_baseline: [-0.02, 0.01]\n- delta_tau_p_value: [0.1, 0.8]\n- theta_rmse_reduction_vs_sequential: [-0.05, 0.05]\n- confounding_scatter_r: [0.0, 0.25]\n- convergence_rhat_max: [1.05, 1.2]\n- convergence_ess_min: [30, 100]\n- stat_validation_corr: [0.15, 0.45]\n- ei_pipeline_internal_consistency: [0.1, 0.35]\n\nNow I'm reviewing the planned experiments. The multi-judge data collection focuses on data quality and coverage, while the item exposure estimation pipeline shows weak signals. The GMFRM fitting has convergence issues with weak confounding estimates, and the baseline comparison shows negligible or negative improvements. Real contamination magnitudes fall below 0.3, judge count sensitivity provides minimal differentiation, and the exposure indicator ablation reveals weak signals with low consistency across the pipeline.\n\nFor the remaining experiments, I'm seeing misfitting confounding estimates and some convergence problems. The confounding observation experiment is already running, the WildBench replication shows the same weak patterns, adversarial robustness is either not applicable or weak, and error propagation sensitivity is already complete. I need to focus on the ones marked as planned in the experiment plan—that's experiments 001 through 011, excluding the running and completed ones. Now I'm generating comprehensive predicted results for the worst-performing tier.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":25617,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":27,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"025abe8b-2d2c-421e-a4ec-955a56bb312b","uuid":"f999877e-3642-4bee-8d17-32d3ec7038d6"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_015gHqfM6mkxUtLymn7YcrSa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntier: worst\nnarrative_guidance: \"Real contamination is negligible on current benchmarks — G-MFRM cannot improve rankings because there is nothing substantial to correct. Pivot to: (1) G-MFRM as principled diagnostic audit framework, (2) the null finding itself as contribution challenging contamination assumptions, (3) identifiability/methodology contribution independent of effect size. Target ARR Findings or AAAI evaluation workshop.\"\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"multi_judge_data_collection\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-001\"\n    description: \"Collect multi-judge evaluation data on MT-Bench and Arena-Hard with 8 judges × 20+ target models\"\n    expected_results:\n      total_judges_collected: 8\n      total_target_models: 22\n      total_judgments_mtbench: 14080\n      total_judgments_arenahard: 35200\n      position_swap_agreement: 0.72\n      inter_judge_agreement_fleiss_kappa: 0.38\n      judge_severity_range_raw: 0.85\n      missing_data_pct: 3.2\n      api_cost_usd: 870\n      gpu_hours_used: 118\n    result_rationale: \"Data collection itself is scenario-independent — the judges produce scores regardless of whether contamination exists. However, under worst tier the inter-judge agreement is moderate (κ≈0.38) because judges disagree primarily on difficulty/style rather than contamination-driven inflation. Position swap agreement is typical (0.72) indicating normal positional bias. The raw judge severity range (0.85 logits) is present but moderate — some judges (e.g., Qwen3.6-27B) show compressed scoring ranges (<0.3 logit range), limiting MFRM's value for those facets.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Judge Collection Summary\"\n        columns: [\"Judge\", \"N_judgments\", \"Mean_score\", \"SD\", \"Severity_range\", \"Position_bias\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"GPT-5.5\", 4400, 7.82, 1.21, \"ref\", 0.08]\n          - [\"Claude Opus 4.7\", 4400, 7.45, 1.35, -0.37, 0.12]\n          - [\"Claude Sonnet 4.6\", 4400, 7.61, 1.18, -0.21, 0.09]\n          - [\"DeepSeek-V4-Pro\", 4400, 7.28, 1.42, -0.54, 0.15]\n          - [\"Qwen3-235B-A22B\", 4400, 7.53, 1.08, -0.29, 0.11]\n          - [\"Qwen3.6-27B\", 4400, 7.91, 0.82, 0.09, 0.06]\n          - [\"Llama 4 Maverick\", 4400, 7.15, 1.48, -0.67, 0.14]\n          - [\"Gemini 2.5 Pro\", 4400, 7.68, 1.25, -0.14, 0.10]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Score distribution heatmap (judges × models): relatively uniform inflation pattern across models — no clear contamination-driven 'spikes' for specific model-item pairs\"\n    analysis: \"Data quality is adequate for MFRM fitting but the score distributions already hint at worst-tier outcome: no judge shows dramatically different severity for known-contaminated vs clean models. The compressed scoring range of Qwen3.6-27B (SD=0.82) will limit its contribution to facet separation.\"\n\n  - name: \"item_exposure_estimation\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-002\"\n    description: \"Estimate per-item exposure e_i via three-source pipeline (performance gap, temporal, LogProber)\"\n    expected_results:\n      ei_mean: 0.18\n      ei_median: 0.12\n      ei_std: 0.22\n      ei_max: 0.71\n      ei_iqr: [0.04, 0.28]\n      pct_items_ei_above_0.5: 8.5\n      performance_gap_signal_snr: 1.2\n      temporal_signal_coverage_pct: 35\n      logprober_coverage_pct: 45\n      source_intercorrelation_gap_temporal: 0.28\n      source_intercorrelation_gap_logprober: 0.31\n      source_intercorrelation_temporal_logprober: 0.19\n      weighted_composite_internal_consistency_alpha: 0.24\n      spline_correction_r2: 0.42\n      reference_variant_quality_check_pass_pct: 78\n    result_rationale: \"Under worst tier, e_i pipeline produces weak/noisy signal (effective σ_e ≈ 0.3–0.5 in the core assumption). Most items show near-zero exposure because current benchmarks (MT-Bench 2024 refresh, Arena-Hard v2) were designed to resist memorization. The three sources have low intercorrelation (0.19–0.31) indicating they capture different noise rather than converging on a true signal. Internal consistency α=0.24 is far below psychometric acceptability (0.70). The few items with e_i > 0.5 are likely legacy items from MT-Bench v1 that survived into the refresh. LogProber coverage is limited (45%) because many target models lack logprob APIs. The spline difficulty correction only explains 42% of variance in raw performance gaps — most of the gap is difficulty-driven, not contamination-driven.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Exposure Source Contribution\"\n        columns: [\"Source\", \"Coverage_pct\", \"Mean_signal\", \"SD\", \"SNR\", \"Correlation_with_composite\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Performance gap (primary)\", 100, 0.15, 0.19, 1.2, 0.78]\n          - [\"Temporal signal\", 35, 0.21, 0.31, 0.8, 0.52]\n          - [\"LogProber\", 45, 0.14, 0.24, 0.9, 0.61]\n      - name: \"e_i Distribution by Benchmark\"\n        columns: [\"Benchmark\", \"N_items\", \"Median_ei\", \"Mean_ei\", \"Pct_above_0.5\", \"Max_ei\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"MT-Bench\", 80, 0.14, 0.20, 10.0, 0.71]\n          - [\"Arena-Hard\", 500, 0.11, 0.17, 7.8, 0.63]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"e_i histogram: heavily right-skewed with mode near 0, long thin tail — most items effectively unexposed\"\n      - \"Source agreement scatter: three panels showing weak positive correlations between each pair of sources, large scatter\"\n    analysis: \"The e_i pipeline's internal consistency (α=0.24) is a critical weakness. The signal is real but noisy — identifiable in aggregate but unreliable at per-item level. This means Stage 3 fitting will have limited power to separate c_n from noise in e_i. The low median (0.12) confirms that most benchmark items are effectively clean, leaving little contamination signal for G-MFRM to exploit.\"\n\n  - name: \"gmfrm_real_data_fit\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-003\"\n    description: \"Fit G-MFRM three-stage pipeline on real data; compare fair scores with Chatbot Arena (Kendall-τ)\"\n    expected_results:\n      stage1_mfrm_convergence_rhat_max: 1.02\n      stage1_mfrm_ess_min: 312\n      stage3_joint_convergence_rhat_max: 1.12\n      stage3_joint_ess_min: 58\n      stage3_cn_rhat_max: 1.18\n      stage3_cn_ess_min: 34\n      kendall_tau_arena_gmfrm_mtbench: 0.76\n      kendall_tau_arena_gmfrm_arenahard: 0.78\n      kendall_tau_arena_mfrm_only_mtbench: 0.75\n      kendall_tau_arena_mfrm_only_arenahard: 0.77\n      cn_median_fitted: 0.15\n      cn_mean_fitted: 0.22\n      cn_max_fitted: 0.61\n      cn_95ci_width_median: 0.48\n      theta_fair_vs_theta_mfrm_rank_correlation: 0.97\n      pct_models_rank_change_geq_2: 9.1\n      divergent_transitions_pct: 2.8\n      runtime_hours: 8.5\n    result_rationale: \"Under worst tier, real c_n distribution has median < 0.3 (fitted median=0.15). Most models have near-zero contamination susceptibility — the c_n posterior piles up near the half-Cauchy prior's mode. The few models with c_n > 0.4 have wide credible intervals (median CI width=0.48) because e_i signal is weak. Stage 3 convergence is problematic: R̂=1.12 overall and up to 1.18 for c_n parameters specifically, with ESS as low as 34 for some c_n. This is because the data provides minimal information about c_n when e_i is near-zero for most items — the likelihood surface is nearly flat in the c_n direction. The resulting fair scores θ̂ barely differ from MFRM-only θ̂ (rank correlation 0.97), meaning the contamination correction has negligible practical effect. Kendall-τ with Arena is 0.76–0.78, within the expected range for this tier but not meaningfully better than MFRM-only.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"G-MFRM Fit Summary by Benchmark\"\n        columns: [\"Benchmark\", \"R_hat_max\", \"ESS_min\", \"Divergent_pct\", \"cn_median\", \"cn_max\", \"tau_Arena\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"MT-Bench\", 1.12, 58, 2.8, 0.17, 0.61, 0.76]\n          - [\"Arena-Hard\", 1.08, 72, 1.5, 0.14, 0.53, 0.78]\n      - name: \"Fitted c_n Distribution (Top 5 Models)\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"c_n_mean\", \"c_n_95CI_low\", \"c_n_95CI_high\", \"CI_width\", \"R_hat\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Model_A (suspected legacy)\", 0.61, 0.18, 1.12, 0.94, 1.15]\n          - [\"Model_B\", 0.43, 0.08, 0.89, 0.81, 1.18]\n          - [\"Model_C\", 0.35, 0.05, 0.74, 0.69, 1.11]\n          - [\"Model_D\", 0.22, 0.01, 0.58, 0.57, 1.09]\n          - [\"Model_E\", 0.19, 0.01, 0.52, 0.51, 1.07]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"c_n posterior density plot: most posteriors overlap heavily with zero; only 2-3 models have posterior mass clearly above 0.3\"\n      - \"θ_fair vs θ_mfrm scatter: nearly perfect diagonal, with 2-3 outliers shifted slightly downward (the high-c_n models)\"\n      - \"Trace plots for c_n parameters: visible autocorrelation, slow mixing for models with c_n near boundary\"\n    analysis: \"Stage 1 MFRM fits well (R̂=1.02) — judge severity and item difficulty are well-identified. But Stage 3 struggles with c_n: the combination of weak e_i signal and low true contamination means the data cannot distinguish c_n=0 from c_n=0.3 for most models. The 2.8% divergent transitions on MT-Bench indicate the posterior geometry is problematic near the c_n≈0 boundary. Practically, fair scores θ̂ are indistinguishable from MFRM-only estimates for 20/22 models. The G-MFRM 'correction' affects only 2-3 models with possible legacy contamination, and even those corrections are within posterior uncertainty.\"\n\n  - name: \"baseline_comparison\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-004\"\n    description: \"Compare G-MFRM against 8 baselines; Kendall-τ and Spearman-ρ with Chatbot Arena; rank change analysis\"\n    expected_results:\n      kendall_tau_raw_average: 0.74\n      kendall_tau_mfrm_only: 0.77\n      kendall_tau_sequential: 0.76\n      kendall_tau_cyclicjudge: 0.78\n      kendall_tau_cyclicjudge_discard: 0.77\n      kendall_tau_dcr: 0.75\n      kendall_tau_uda: 0.79\n      kendall_tau_prefleak_calib: 0.76\n      kendall_tau_gmfrm: 0.77\n      best_baseline: \"UDA\"\n      delta_tau_vs_best_baseline: -0.02\n      delta_tau_vs_mfrm_only: 0.00\n      wilcoxon_p_gmfrm_vs_uda: 0.34\n      wilcoxon_p_gmfrm_vs_cyclicjudge: 0.62\n      spearman_rho_gmfrm: 0.89\n      spearman_rho_best_baseline: 0.91\n      rank_change_models_geq2_positions: 2\n      top5_stability_gmfrm: 0.80\n      top5_stability_best_baseline: 0.80\n      post_hoc_power_for_observed_delta: 0.12\n      n_models: 22\n    result_rationale: \"Under worst tier, Δτ is in [-0.02, 0.01] range. G-MFRM (τ=0.77) is actually slightly BELOW UDA (τ=0.79) — UDA's neural K-factor adjustment captures judge-specific biases that are the dominant source of ranking noise when contamination is negligible. CyclicJudge (τ=0.78) also edges out G-MFRM because round-robin bias elimination is effective when contamination is near-zero. The Wilcoxon test is massively underpowered (power=0.12 at N=22 models for detecting Δτ=0.02) — we cannot distinguish methods statistically. G-MFRM ties MFRM-only (both τ=0.77), confirming the contamination correction adds nothing when there's nothing to correct. Rank changes are minimal: only 2 models shift ≥2 positions between G-MFRM and best baseline, and these are within posterior uncertainty. This is the fatal result for Claim 3.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Main Comparison Table\"\n        columns: [\"Method\", \"Kendall_tau\", \"Spearman_rho\", \"Rank_changes_geq2\", \"Top5_stable\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Raw multi-judge average\", 0.74, 0.86, \"ref\", \"ref\"]\n          - [\"MFRM-only (Stage 1)\", 0.77, 0.89, 3, \"4/5\"]\n          - [\"Sequential (MFRM→contamination)\", 0.76, 0.88, 2, \"4/5\"]\n          - [\"CyclicJudge\", 0.78, 0.90, 3, \"4/5\"]\n          - [\"CyclicJudge + item discard\", 0.77, 0.89, 2, \"4/5\"]\n          - [\"DCR Factor\", 0.75, 0.87, 2, \"4/5\"]\n          - [\"UDA\", 0.79, 0.91, 4, \"4/5\"]\n          - [\"Preference Leakage calibration\", 0.76, 0.88, 2, \"4/5\"]\n          - [\"G-MFRM (ours)\", 0.77, 0.89, 2, \"4/5\"]\n      - name: \"Statistical Tests\"\n        columns: [\"Comparison\", \"Delta_tau\", \"Wilcoxon_p\", \"Power_at_alpha_0.05\", \"Significant?\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"G-MFRM vs UDA\", -0.02, 0.34, 0.12, \"No\"]\n          - [\"G-MFRM vs CyclicJudge\", -0.01, 0.62, 0.08, \"No\"]\n          - [\"G-MFRM vs MFRM-only\", 0.00, 0.91, 0.05, \"No\"]\n          - [\"G-MFRM vs Sequential\", 0.01, 0.55, 0.09, \"No\"]\n          - [\"G-MFRM vs Raw average\", 0.03, 0.18, 0.22, \"No\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Bootstrap confidence interval plot: all methods' τ CIs overlap heavily; G-MFRM CI centered at 0.77 with [0.71, 0.83] — indistinguishable from 5 other methods\"\n      - \"Rank change alluvial diagram: nearly static rankings across methods; 2-3 models shift 1-2 positions, driven by judge bias correction not contamination\"\n    analysis: \"Claim 3 is dead. G-MFRM does not improve over best baselines — it slightly underperforms UDA. Post-hoc power analysis (power=0.12) confirms the study is fundamentally underpowered to detect the observed effect size with N=22 models. Even with 50 models, power would be ~0.25 for Δτ=0.02. The ranking improvement is non-significant by any standard. MFRM-only and G-MFRM produce identical τ, confirming the contamination gating mechanism adds no signal. Pivot narrative: G-MFRM 'degrades gracefully' to MFRM level when contamination is absent — this is a feature, not a failure.\"\n\n  - name: \"contamination_magnitude_calibration\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-005\"\n    description: \"Estimate real contamination magnitude; determine if c_n matches mvp_001 (weak) or mvp_002 (strong)\"\n    expected_results:\n      real_cn_median: 0.15\n      real_cn_mean: 0.22\n      real_cn_max: 0.61\n      real_cn_iqr: [0.04, 0.32]\n      pct_models_cn_above_0.5: 9.1\n      pct_models_cn_above_1.0: 0.0\n      closest_mvp_match: \"below mvp_001\"\n      mvp_001_cn_mean_was: 0.5\n      ratio_real_to_mvp001: 0.44\n      constat_delta_correlation_with_cn: 0.32\n      constat_delta_median: 0.08\n      cn_distribution_shape: \"heavily right-skewed, mode near 0\"\n      bimodality_coefficient: 0.31\n    result_rationale: \"Under worst tier, real c_n median is in [0.05, 0.3] — we get 0.15, which is BELOW even mvp_001's assumption of c~0.5. The distribution is not bimodal but heavily right-skewed with mode near zero: most models have negligible contamination, 2-3 have moderate (0.4-0.6), none reach 1.0. This is well below the regime where G-MFRM's gating mechanism has meaningful leverage. The ConStat per-model delta validates only weakly (r=0.32) because both signals are near-floor — correlation is attenuated by floor effects and measurement noise. The finding itself IS informative: current benchmarks (MT-Bench 2024 refresh, Arena-Hard v2) appear to have effectively low contamination. This challenges assumptions in contamination literature that training data overlap produces large performance inflation on these specific benchmarks.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Contamination Magnitude Comparison\"\n        columns: [\"Source\", \"cn_median\", \"cn_mean\", \"cn_max\", \"Interpretation\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"MVP synthetic (mvp_001)\", 0.50, 0.50, 1.00, \"Assumed moderate\"]\n          - [\"MVP synthetic (mvp_002/003)\", 1.50, 1.50, 2.50, \"Assumed strong\"]\n          - [\"Real data (this experiment)\", 0.15, 0.22, 0.61, \"Negligible to weak\"]\n      - name: \"Per-Model c_n Estimates\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"c_n_posterior_mean\", \"95CI\", \"ConStat_delta\", \"Category\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"GPT-5.5\", 0.08, \"[0.01, 0.29]\", 0.05, \"negligible\"]\n          - [\"Claude Opus 4.7\", 0.05, \"[0.00, 0.21]\", 0.03, \"negligible\"]\n          - [\"Llama 4 405B\", 0.35, \"[0.06, 0.72]\", 0.15, \"weak\"]\n          - [\"Qwen3-235B\", 0.43, \"[0.08, 0.89]\", 0.18, \"weak-moderate\"]\n          - [\"DeepSeek-V4\", 0.61, \"[0.18, 1.12]\", 0.22, \"moderate (max)\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"c_n distribution: kernel density showing heavy pile-up near 0, thin tail to 0.6; overlaid with mvp_001 and mvp_002 assumed distributions for visual comparison of how much weaker real signal is\"\n      - \"ConStat delta vs G-MFRM c_n scatter: weak positive trend (r=0.32), large scatter, most points clustered near origin\"\n    analysis: \"This is the definitive worst-tier signal. Real contamination is far below synthetic assumptions. The mvp_002/003 success (43.8% RMSE reduction) required c_n~1.5 — real data has c_n~0.15 (10× weaker). Even mvp_001 assumed c~0.5. The G-MFRM's gating mechanism is solving a problem that barely exists on these benchmarks. Pivot implications: (1) the null finding about contamination magnitude is itself publishable — benchmarks may be cleaner than assumed; (2) G-MFRM's value is as a diagnostic tool that CONFIRMS low contamination rather than correcting for high contamination; (3) need to find or construct a benchmark with known high contamination for validation case.\"\n\n  - name: \"judge_count_sensitivity\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-006\"\n    description: \"Vary judges (2,4,6,8); measure G-MFRM degradation; leave-one-judge-out residual diagnostic\"\n    expected_results:\n      tau_8_judges: 0.77\n      tau_6_judges: 0.76\n      tau_4_judges: 0.75\n      tau_2_judges: 0.73\n      tau_mfrm_only_8_judges: 0.77\n      tau_mfrm_only_4_judges: 0.75\n      delta_gmfrm_vs_mfrm_8j: 0.00\n      delta_gmfrm_vs_mfrm_6j: 0.00\n      delta_gmfrm_vs_mfrm_4j: 0.00\n      delta_gmfrm_vs_mfrm_2j: -0.01\n      cn_identifiability_threshold_judges: \"not reached\"\n      leave_one_out_max_residual_change: 0.04\n      leave_one_out_same_family_bias_detected: false\n      convergence_2j_rhat_max: 1.25\n      convergence_4j_rhat_max: 1.15\n      convergence_6j_rhat_max: 1.10\n    result_rationale: \"Under worst tier, G-MFRM never outperforms MFRM-only at any judge count because contamination is negligible — the gating mechanism adds parameters without adding signal. At 2 judges, G-MFRM actually slightly underperforms MFRM-only (Δτ=-0.01) due to over-parameterization with insufficient data. Convergence degrades progressively as judges decrease (R̂=1.25 at 2 judges) because c_n parameters are completely unidentifiable with sparse judge coverage and weak e_i. The leave-one-judge-out diagnostic shows no systematic same-family bias pattern — residual changes are small (max 0.04) because contamination effects are too weak to create detectable judge-model interactions. The practical conclusion is that with ≥4 judges, G-MFRM is harmless (degrades gracefully to MFRM level) but never beneficial for ranking.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Judge Count Sensitivity\"\n        columns: [\"N_judges\", \"tau_GMFRM\", \"tau_MFRM\", \"Delta\", \"R_hat_max\", \"ESS_min_cn\"]\n        rows:\n          - [8, 0.77, 0.77, 0.00, 1.12, 58]\n          - [6, 0.76, 0.76, 0.00, 1.10, 65]\n          - [4, 0.75, 0.75, 0.00, 1.15, 42]\n          - [2, 0.73, 0.74, -0.01, 1.25, 22]\n      - name: \"Leave-One-Judge-Out Residual Diagnostic\"\n        columns: [\"Removed_judge\", \"Family_overlap?\", \"Max_residual_change\", \"Systematic_bias?\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"GPT-5.5\", \"Yes (GPT family targets)\", 0.04, \"No\"]\n          - [\"Claude Opus 4.7\", \"Yes (Claude targets)\", 0.03, \"No\"]\n          - [\"DeepSeek-V4-Pro\", \"Yes (DeepSeek targets)\", 0.02, \"No\"]\n          - [\"Qwen3-235B-A22B\", \"Yes (Qwen targets)\", 0.03, \"No\"]\n          - [\"Llama 4 Maverick\", \"Yes (Llama targets)\", 0.02, \"No\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Performance degradation curve: two nearly overlapping lines (G-MFRM and MFRM-only) both declining gently from 8→2 judges; gap is zero or negative throughout\"\n      - \"R̂ vs judge count: exponential increase below 4 judges for c_n parameters specifically, while θ and d parameters remain well-behaved\"\n    analysis: \"This experiment confirms G-MFRM adds nothing over MFRM-only at any judge count in the worst tier. The contamination facet is unidentifiable in practice. However, the graceful degradation finding is publishable as a safety property: G-MFRM doesn't HARM rankings when contamination is absent. The leave-one-judge-out diagnostic is useful as a methodological contribution regardless of contamination magnitude — it provides a principled way to detect preference leakage without requiring contamination to be present.\"\n\n  - name: \"exposure_indicator_ablation\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-007\"\n    description: \"Ablation: contribution of each exposure source to e_i quality; leave-one-out analysis\"\n    expected_results:\n      full_pipeline_alpha: 0.24\n      leave_out_performance_gap_alpha: 0.15\n      leave_out_temporal_alpha: 0.22\n      leave_out_logprober_alpha: 0.20\n      performance_gap_unique_contribution: 0.09\n      temporal_unique_contribution: 0.02\n      logprober_unique_contribution: 0.04\n      performance_gap_only_tau: 0.76\n      temporal_only_tau: 0.75\n      logprober_only_tau: 0.75\n      full_pipeline_tau: 0.77\n      no_ei_mfrm_only_tau: 0.77\n      delta_any_source_vs_no_ei: [-0.02, 0.01]\n    result_rationale: \"Under worst tier, e_i pipeline has internal consistency α=0.24 (floor). When we ablate sources, the full pipeline (α=0.24) is already so weak that removing any single source barely changes downstream τ. The performance gap is the strongest single source but still weak (unique contribution = 0.09 to α). Temporal and LogProber add negligible incremental signal. Critically, ALL configurations of e_i produce the same downstream τ (0.75–0.77) because the contamination correction itself is inconsequential — varying e_i quality doesn't matter when c_n ≈ 0. The 'no e_i' condition (MFRM-only, τ=0.77) matches or beats all e_i conditions, confirming the entire exposure estimation pipeline is effort without payoff on these benchmarks.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Exposure Source Ablation\"\n        columns: [\"Configuration\", \"Alpha\", \"e_i_mean\", \"e_i_SD\", \"Downstream_tau\", \"Delta_vs_MFRM_only\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Full pipeline (3 sources)\", 0.24, 0.18, 0.22, 0.77, 0.00]\n          - [\"Without performance gap\", 0.15, 0.16, 0.20, 0.76, -0.01]\n          - [\"Without temporal\", 0.22, 0.17, 0.21, 0.77, 0.00]\n          - [\"Without LogProber\", 0.20, 0.19, 0.23, 0.76, -0.01]\n          - [\"Performance gap only\", \"N/A\", 0.15, 0.19, 0.76, -0.01]\n          - [\"No e_i (MFRM-only)\", \"N/A\", \"N/A\", \"N/A\", 0.77, \"ref\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Bar chart of unique variance contribution per source: all bars near zero, performance gap slightly taller but still below meaningful threshold\"\n      - \"Downstream τ forest plot: all configurations' CIs overlap completely, including MFRM-only baseline\"\n    analysis: \"The ablation confirms that no e_i source meaningfully contributes to ranking quality under worst tier. This is not a pipeline failure per se — it's that the signal being measured (item exposure) is genuinely near-zero on these benchmarks. Pivot narrative: the pipeline is VALIDATED for measuring exposure (internal structure exists, α=0.24 > 0), but the underlying construct is at floor. This supports the 'benchmarks are clean' narrative contribution.\"\n\n  - name: \"facet_fit_diagnostics\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-008\"\n    description: \"Measurement quality: infit/outfit, person-fit, divergent transitions, posterior predictive checks\"\n    expected_results:\n      theta_infit_mean: 1.02\n      theta_outfit_mean: 1.05\n      theta_misfit_pct: 4.5\n      b_infit_mean: 0.99\n      b_outfit_mean: 1.03\n      b_misfit_pct: 6.2\n      d_infit_mean: 1.01\n      d_outfit_mean: 1.04\n      d_misfit_pct: 12.5\n      cn_infit_mean: 1.15\n      cn_outfit_mean: 1.28\n      cn_misfit_pct: 36.4\n      divergent_transitions_total: 142\n      divergent_transitions_pct: 2.2\n      posterior_predictive_p_global: 0.41\n      posterior_predictive_p_items_below_0.05_pct: 8.3\n      lz_stage1_vs_cn_stage3_correlation: 0.18\n      stage1_emri_flagged_models: 3\n      cn_flagged_same_models: 1\n    result_rationale: \"Under worst tier, θ, b, and d facets fit well because MFRM structure is appropriate for multi-judge data (these are just standard psychometric facets). But c_n shows substantial misfit: 36.4% of models have outfit > 1.3, indicating the rank-1 contamination model (c_n·e_i) doesn't describe the residual patterns. This is because when true contamination is near-zero, the c_n parameters are fitting noise rather than signal — any nonzero c_n estimate absorbs heterogeneous residual variance, producing outfit. The lz (person-fit) from Stage 1 correlates only weakly (r=0.18) with c_n from Stage 3, meaning the two stages are identifying different 'aberrant' patterns — further evidence that c_n is not measuring a coherent construct on this data. Divergent transitions (2.2%) cluster around c_n parameters near the half-Cauchy boundary. The model fits well OVERALL (global posterior predictive p=0.41) because c_n terms are near-zero and the model is effectively MFRM.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Facet Fit Summary\"\n        columns: [\"Facet\", \"N\", \"Infit_mean\", \"Outfit_mean\", \"Misfit_pct\", \"Interpretation\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"theta (ability)\", 22, 1.02, 1.05, 4.5, \"Good fit\"]\n          - [\"b (difficulty)\", 580, 0.99, 1.03, 6.2, \"Good fit\"]\n          - [\"d (severity)\", 8, 1.01, 1.04, 12.5, \"Acceptable (1/8 judges borderline)\"]\n          - [\"c_n (contamination)\", 22, 1.15, 1.28, 36.4, \"Poor fit — construct at floor\"]\n      - name: \"Convergence Diagnostics Detail\"\n        columns: [\"Parameter_group\", \"R_hat_median\", \"R_hat_max\", \"ESS_median\", \"ESS_min\", \"Divergent_pct\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"theta\", 1.01, 1.03, 892, 412, 0.1]\n          - [\"b\", 1.00, 1.02, 1105, 523, 0.2]\n          - [\"d\", 1.01, 1.02, 945, 678, 0.1]\n          - [\"c_n\", 1.05, 1.18, 156, 34, 1.8]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Wright map (person-item) from Stage 1: well-calibrated, items span ability range adequately\"\n      - \"c_n outfit vs c_n posterior mean: negative trend — models with higher c_n have WORSE fit, suggesting noise-fitting\"\n      - \"Trace plots for 3 worst-mixing c_n parameters: visible stuck chains, slow drift\"\n    analysis: \"Diagnostics reveal a split personality: G-MFRM as MFRM (θ,b,d) works well; the contamination extension (c_n) is poorly identified and misfitting. This is consistent with worst tier where c_n attempts to model a near-absent signal. The 36.4% c_n misfit rate would normally indicate model failure, but reframed: it shows the contamination term is unnecessary for this data. Pivot value: these diagnostics themselves demonstrate G-MFRM's utility as a diagnostic framework — the misfit detection tells practitioners 'contamination is not a concern on this benchmark,' which is actionable information.\"\n\n  - name: \"confounding_formal_observation\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-009\"\n    description: \"Formal OVB bias derivation + Monte Carlo validation across realistic parameter ranges; synthetic c_n recovery\"\n    expected_results:\n      bias_formula_derived: true\n      monte_carlo_n_conditions: 48\n      monte_carlo_reps_per_condition: 100\n      bias_magnitude_at_cn_0.15: 0.02\n      bias_magnitude_at_cn_0.5: 0.08\n      bias_magnitude_at_cn_1.5: 0.31\n      bias_direction_prediction_accuracy: 0.94\n      cn_recovery_rmse_at_real_magnitude: 0.42\n      cn_recovery_95ci_coverage_at_real: 0.78\n      cn_recovery_rmse_at_mvp_magnitude: 0.18\n      cn_recovery_95ci_coverage_at_mvp: 0.91\n      confounding_scatter_r_at_real_cn: 0.12\n      confounding_scatter_r_at_mvp_cn: 0.65\n      orthogonality_test_real_data: 0.09\n    result_rationale: \"The formal OVB observation is valid regardless of contamination magnitude — it's a mathematical identity. Monte Carlo confirms: bias direction prediction is 94% accurate across all conditions. BUT the practical bias magnitude scales linearly with c_n. At real-data contamination levels (c_n≈0.15), the sequential estimator bias is only 0.02 logits — negligible relative to posterior uncertainty. The confounding scatter r drops from 0.65 (mvp_002/003 regime) to 0.12 (real-data regime) because d_j and c_n·e_i are approximately orthogonal when c_n is near-zero. Synthetic c_n recovery shows the method WORKS at high contamination (RMSE=0.18, coverage=0.91 at mvp levels) but FAILS at real-data levels (RMSE=0.42, coverage=0.78) — the posterior is too wide to be informative. This confirms the core assumption: 'd_j and c_n·e_i approximately orthogonal in this dataset' — confounding exists in theory but not in practice.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Monte Carlo Bias Magnitude by c_n Level\"\n        columns: [\"c_n_regime\", \"Bias_theta_RMSE\", \"Scatter_r\", \"Sequential_degradation_pct\", \"Practical_significance\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"c_n = 0.15 (real)\", 0.02, 0.12, 1.8, \"Negligible\"]\n          - [\"c_n = 0.50 (mvp_001)\", 0.08, 0.38, 12.5, \"Marginal\"]\n          - [\"c_n = 1.00\", 0.19, 0.52, 28.3, \"Moderate\"]\n          - [\"c_n = 1.50 (mvp_002/003)\", 0.31, 0.65, 43.8, \"Large\"]\n      - name: \"Synthetic c_n Recovery at Real-Data Magnitude\"\n        columns: [\"Metric\", \"c_n=0.15\", \"c_n=0.50\", \"c_n=1.50\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"c_n RMSE\", 0.42, 0.28, 0.18]\n          - [\"95% CI coverage\", 0.78, 0.87, 0.91]\n          - [\"c_n correlation (true vs fitted)\", 0.35, 0.72, 0.92]\n          - [\"Pct models correctly ranked by c_n\", 0.55, 0.78, 0.94]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Bias magnitude surface plot: x=c_n level, y=rho(d,c·e), z=theta bias — steep only in high-c_n, high-rho corner\"\n      - \"Confounding scatter at 4 c_n levels: panel plot showing scatter tightening to diagonal as c_n decreases\"\n    analysis: \"The formal observation remains a valid theoretical contribution — OVB exists regardless of magnitude. But the practical punchline under worst tier: the bias is negligible (0.02 logits) because contamination is too low to create meaningful confounding. The synthetic c_n recovery at real-data magnitude (correlation 0.35) confirms G-MFRM cannot reliably rank models by contamination susceptibility when the signal is this weak. Pivot framing: 'we derive the confounding mechanism and show it activates under conditions X; current benchmarks happen to NOT meet these conditions; the correction is available when needed.'\"\n\n  - name: \"wildbench_replication\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-010\"\n    description: \"Replicate G-MFRM pipeline on WildBench as third benchmark for cross-benchmark generalization\"\n    expected_results:\n      wildbench_n_items: 1024\n      wildbench_n_judges: 6\n      wildbench_kendall_tau_gmfrm: 0.74\n      wildbench_kendall_tau_mfrm: 0.75\n      wildbench_kendall_tau_uda: 0.76\n      wildbench_delta_tau_gmfrm_vs_best: -0.02\n      wildbench_cn_median: 0.09\n      wildbench_cn_max: 0.38\n      wildbench_ei_median: 0.08\n      wildbench_convergence_rhat_max: 1.14\n      wildbench_convergence_ess_min: 41\n      cross_benchmark_cn_correlation: 0.28\n      cross_benchmark_theta_correlation: 0.92\n    result_rationale: \"WildBench uses more diverse, harder items sourced from real user interactions — these items are LESS likely to be contaminated than MT-Bench/Arena-Hard because they're more recent and less standardized. Under worst tier, WildBench shows even lower contamination (median c_n=0.09) than the other benchmarks. G-MFRM slightly underperforms both MFRM-only and UDA because the contamination parameters add noise. Cross-benchmark c_n correlation is weak (r=0.28) — models don't show consistent contamination patterns across benchmarks, suggesting what little contamination exists is benchmark-specific (item-level memorization) rather than model-level training data overlap. Cross-benchmark θ correlation (0.92) confirms ability estimates are stable regardless of contamination correction method.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Cross-Benchmark Comparison\"\n        columns: [\"Benchmark\", \"tau_GMFRM\", \"tau_MFRM\", \"tau_UDA\", \"cn_median\", \"ei_median\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"MT-Bench\", 0.76, 0.75, 0.78, 0.17, 0.14]\n          - [\"Arena-Hard\", 0.78, 0.77, 0.79, 0.14, 0.11]\n          - [\"WildBench\", 0.74, 0.75, 0.76, 0.09, 0.08]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cross-benchmark c_n scatter matrix: 3 panels showing weak correlations between benchmarks — contamination is benchmark-specific, not model-intrinsic\"\n    analysis: \"WildBench replication confirms worst-tier pattern generalizes: negligible contamination, no ranking benefit from G-MFRM. The low cross-benchmark c_n correlation (0.28) suggests that contamination — what little exists — is item-specific rather than reflecting stable model-level susceptibility. This weakens the c_n construct validity further but supports the 'benchmarks are clean' narrative.\"\n\n  - name: \"adversarial_robustness\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-011\"\n    description: \"Test G-MFRM robustness against adversarial contamination following Wang et al. (ICLR 2026)\"\n    expected_results:\n      adversarial_injection_levels: [0.0, 0.3, 0.5, 1.0, 1.5]\n      tau_gmfrm_at_0.0: 0.77\n      tau_gmfrm_at_0.3: 0.76\n      tau_gmfrm_at_0.5: 0.78\n      tau_gmfrm_at_1.0: 0.82\n      tau_gmfrm_at_1.5: 0.87\n      tau_sequential_at_1.5: 0.79\n      tau_mfrm_at_1.5: 0.75\n      crossover_point_cn: 0.7\n      cn_recovery_at_injection_1.5: 0.81\n      detection_power_at_real_cn: 0.15\n      detection_power_at_injected_1.5: 0.92\n    result_rationale: \"Under worst tier, natural contamination is negligible — but we can INJECT synthetic contamination to demonstrate G-MFRM works when contamination IS present. By artificially inflating scores for specific models on high-e_i items, we create a controlled setting. At injection level 1.5 (matching mvp_002/003), G-MFRM recovers well (τ=0.87 vs sequential's 0.79). The crossover point where G-MFRM starts outperforming is c_n≈0.7 — above real-data levels but demonstrating the method's potential. Detection power at real c_n levels is only 0.15 (cannot detect what isn't there), but jumps to 0.92 at injected 1.5. This experiment redeems the method theoretically while confirming it's unnecessary on current benchmarks.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Adversarial Injection Results\"\n        columns: [\"Injection_level\", \"tau_GMFRM\", \"tau_Sequential\", \"tau_MFRM\", \"cn_recovery_corr\", \"Detection_power\"]\n        rows:\n          - [0.0, 0.77, 0.76, 0.77, 0.35, 0.15]\n          - [0.3, 0.76, 0.76, 0.76, 0.42, 0.25]\n          - [0.5, 0.78, 0.77, 0.76, 0.58, 0.45]\n          - [1.0, 0.82, 0.79, 0.75, 0.74, 0.78]\n          - [1.5, 0.87, 0.79, 0.75, 0.81, 0.92]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Crossover plot: G-MFRM vs baselines as function of injected contamination level — lines cross around c_n=0.7, with G-MFRM pulling ahead in high-contamination regime\"\n      - \"Annotated vertical line at 'real data level' (c_n≈0.15) showing all methods equivalent there\"\n    analysis: \"Adversarial robustness provides the strongest pivot evidence: G-MFRM works when contamination exists, it just doesn't exist on current benchmarks. The crossover at c_n=0.7 gives a principled threshold for when G-MFRM becomes necessary. Framing: 'G-MFRM is an insurance policy — unnecessary today but protective against future contamination increases as training data grows.' Also validates the methodology for historical benchmarks (e.g., MMLU original) where contamination IS known to be high.\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":25617,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":27,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"025abe8b-2d2c-421e-a4ec-955a56bb312b","uuid":"70e4eba8-60b9-43d1-8f69-396ec73b770f"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":432473,"duration_api_ms":434470,"num_turns":1,"result":"```yaml\ntier: worst\nnarrative_guidance: \"Real contamination is negligible on current benchmarks — G-MFRM cannot improve rankings because there is nothing substantial to correct. Pivot to: (1) G-MFRM as principled diagnostic audit framework, (2) the null finding itself as contribution challenging contamination assumptions, (3) identifiability/methodology contribution independent of effect size. Target ARR Findings or AAAI evaluation workshop.\"\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"multi_judge_data_collection\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-001\"\n    description: \"Collect multi-judge evaluation data on MT-Bench and Arena-Hard with 8 judges × 20+ target models\"\n    expected_results:\n      total_judges_collected: 8\n      total_target_models: 22\n      total_judgments_mtbench: 14080\n      total_judgments_arenahard: 35200\n      position_swap_agreement: 0.72\n      inter_judge_agreement_fleiss_kappa: 0.38\n      judge_severity_range_raw: 0.85\n      missing_data_pct: 3.2\n      api_cost_usd: 870\n      gpu_hours_used: 118\n    result_rationale: \"Data collection itself is scenario-independent — the judges produce scores regardless of whether contamination exists. However, under worst tier the inter-judge agreement is moderate (κ≈0.38) because judges disagree primarily on difficulty/style rather than contamination-driven inflation. Position swap agreement is typical (0.72) indicating normal positional bias. The raw judge severity range (0.85 logits) is present but moderate — some judges (e.g., Qwen3.6-27B) show compressed scoring ranges (<0.3 logit range), limiting MFRM's value for those facets.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Judge Collection Summary\"\n        columns: [\"Judge\", \"N_judgments\", \"Mean_score\", \"SD\", \"Severity_range\", \"Position_bias\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"GPT-5.5\", 4400, 7.82, 1.21, \"ref\", 0.08]\n          - [\"Claude Opus 4.7\", 4400, 7.45, 1.35, -0.37, 0.12]\n          - [\"Claude Sonnet 4.6\", 4400, 7.61, 1.18, -0.21, 0.09]\n          - [\"DeepSeek-V4-Pro\", 4400, 7.28, 1.42, -0.54, 0.15]\n          - [\"Qwen3-235B-A22B\", 4400, 7.53, 1.08, -0.29, 0.11]\n          - [\"Qwen3.6-27B\", 4400, 7.91, 0.82, 0.09, 0.06]\n          - [\"Llama 4 Maverick\", 4400, 7.15, 1.48, -0.67, 0.14]\n          - [\"Gemini 2.5 Pro\", 4400, 7.68, 1.25, -0.14, 0.10]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Score distribution heatmap (judges × models): relatively uniform inflation pattern across models — no clear contamination-driven 'spikes' for specific model-item pairs\"\n    analysis: \"Data quality is adequate for MFRM fitting but the score distributions already hint at worst-tier outcome: no judge shows dramatically different severity for known-contaminated vs clean models. The compressed scoring range of Qwen3.6-27B (SD=0.82) will limit its contribution to facet separation.\"\n\n  - name: \"item_exposure_estimation\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-002\"\n    description: \"Estimate per-item exposure e_i via three-source pipeline (performance gap, temporal, LogProber)\"\n    expected_results:\n      ei_mean: 0.18\n      ei_median: 0.12\n      ei_std: 0.22\n      ei_max: 0.71\n      ei_iqr: [0.04, 0.28]\n      pct_items_ei_above_0.5: 8.5\n      performance_gap_signal_snr: 1.2\n      temporal_signal_coverage_pct: 35\n      logprober_coverage_pct: 45\n      source_intercorrelation_gap_temporal: 0.28\n      source_intercorrelation_gap_logprober: 0.31\n      source_intercorrelation_temporal_logprober: 0.19\n      weighted_composite_internal_consistency_alpha: 0.24\n      spline_correction_r2: 0.42\n      reference_variant_quality_check_pass_pct: 78\n    result_rationale: \"Under worst tier, e_i pipeline produces weak/noisy signal (effective σ_e ≈ 0.3–0.5 in the core assumption). Most items show near-zero exposure because current benchmarks (MT-Bench 2024 refresh, Arena-Hard v2) were designed to resist memorization. The three sources have low intercorrelation (0.19–0.31) indicating they capture different noise rather than converging on a true signal. Internal consistency α=0.24 is far below psychometric acceptability (0.70). The few items with e_i > 0.5 are likely legacy items from MT-Bench v1 that survived into the refresh. LogProber coverage is limited (45%) because many target models lack logprob APIs. The spline difficulty correction only explains 42% of variance in raw performance gaps — most of the gap is difficulty-driven, not contamination-driven.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Exposure Source Contribution\"\n        columns: [\"Source\", \"Coverage_pct\", \"Mean_signal\", \"SD\", \"SNR\", \"Correlation_with_composite\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Performance gap (primary)\", 100, 0.15, 0.19, 1.2, 0.78]\n          - [\"Temporal signal\", 35, 0.21, 0.31, 0.8, 0.52]\n          - [\"LogProber\", 45, 0.14, 0.24, 0.9, 0.61]\n      - name: \"e_i Distribution by Benchmark\"\n        columns: [\"Benchmark\", \"N_items\", \"Median_ei\", \"Mean_ei\", \"Pct_above_0.5\", \"Max_ei\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"MT-Bench\", 80, 0.14, 0.20, 10.0, 0.71]\n          - [\"Arena-Hard\", 500, 0.11, 0.17, 7.8, 0.63]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"e_i histogram: heavily right-skewed with mode near 0, long thin tail — most items effectively unexposed\"\n      - \"Source agreement scatter: three panels showing weak positive correlations between each pair of sources, large scatter\"\n    analysis: \"The e_i pipeline's internal consistency (α=0.24) is a critical weakness. The signal is real but noisy — identifiable in aggregate but unreliable at per-item level. This means Stage 3 fitting will have limited power to separate c_n from noise in e_i. The low median (0.12) confirms that most benchmark items are effectively clean, leaving little contamination signal for G-MFRM to exploit.\"\n\n  - name: \"gmfrm_real_data_fit\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-003\"\n    description: \"Fit G-MFRM three-stage pipeline on real data; compare fair scores with Chatbot Arena (Kendall-τ)\"\n    expected_results:\n      stage1_mfrm_convergence_rhat_max: 1.02\n      stage1_mfrm_ess_min: 312\n      stage3_joint_convergence_rhat_max: 1.12\n      stage3_joint_ess_min: 58\n      stage3_cn_rhat_max: 1.18\n      stage3_cn_ess_min: 34\n      kendall_tau_arena_gmfrm_mtbench: 0.76\n      kendall_tau_arena_gmfrm_arenahard: 0.78\n      kendall_tau_arena_mfrm_only_mtbench: 0.75\n      kendall_tau_arena_mfrm_only_arenahard: 0.77\n      cn_median_fitted: 0.15\n      cn_mean_fitted: 0.22\n      cn_max_fitted: 0.61\n      cn_95ci_width_median: 0.48\n      theta_fair_vs_theta_mfrm_rank_correlation: 0.97\n      pct_models_rank_change_geq_2: 9.1\n      divergent_transitions_pct: 2.8\n      runtime_hours: 8.5\n    result_rationale: \"Under worst tier, real c_n distribution has median < 0.3 (fitted median=0.15). Most models have near-zero contamination susceptibility — the c_n posterior piles up near the half-Cauchy prior's mode. The few models with c_n > 0.4 have wide credible intervals (median CI width=0.48) because e_i signal is weak. Stage 3 convergence is problematic: R̂=1.12 overall and up to 1.18 for c_n parameters specifically, with ESS as low as 34 for some c_n. This is because the data provides minimal information about c_n when e_i is near-zero for most items — the likelihood surface is nearly flat in the c_n direction. The resulting fair scores θ̂ barely differ from MFRM-only θ̂ (rank correlation 0.97), meaning the contamination correction has negligible practical effect. Kendall-τ with Arena is 0.76–0.78, within the expected range for this tier but not meaningfully better than MFRM-only.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"G-MFRM Fit Summary by Benchmark\"\n        columns: [\"Benchmark\", \"R_hat_max\", \"ESS_min\", \"Divergent_pct\", \"cn_median\", \"cn_max\", \"tau_Arena\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"MT-Bench\", 1.12, 58, 2.8, 0.17, 0.61, 0.76]\n          - [\"Arena-Hard\", 1.08, 72, 1.5, 0.14, 0.53, 0.78]\n      - name: \"Fitted c_n Distribution (Top 5 Models)\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"c_n_mean\", \"c_n_95CI_low\", \"c_n_95CI_high\", \"CI_width\", \"R_hat\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Model_A (suspected legacy)\", 0.61, 0.18, 1.12, 0.94, 1.15]\n          - [\"Model_B\", 0.43, 0.08, 0.89, 0.81, 1.18]\n          - [\"Model_C\", 0.35, 0.05, 0.74, 0.69, 1.11]\n          - [\"Model_D\", 0.22, 0.01, 0.58, 0.57, 1.09]\n          - [\"Model_E\", 0.19, 0.01, 0.52, 0.51, 1.07]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"c_n posterior density plot: most posteriors overlap heavily with zero; only 2-3 models have posterior mass clearly above 0.3\"\n      - \"θ_fair vs θ_mfrm scatter: nearly perfect diagonal, with 2-3 outliers shifted slightly downward (the high-c_n models)\"\n      - \"Trace plots for c_n parameters: visible autocorrelation, slow mixing for models with c_n near boundary\"\n    analysis: \"Stage 1 MFRM fits well (R̂=1.02) — judge severity and item difficulty are well-identified. But Stage 3 struggles with c_n: the combination of weak e_i signal and low true contamination means the data cannot distinguish c_n=0 from c_n=0.3 for most models. The 2.8% divergent transitions on MT-Bench indicate the posterior geometry is problematic near the c_n≈0 boundary. Practically, fair scores θ̂ are indistinguishable from MFRM-only estimates for 20/22 models. The G-MFRM 'correction' affects only 2-3 models with possible legacy contamination, and even those corrections are within posterior uncertainty.\"\n\n  - name: \"baseline_comparison\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-004\"\n    description: \"Compare G-MFRM against 8 baselines; Kendall-τ and Spearman-ρ with Chatbot Arena; rank change analysis\"\n    expected_results:\n      kendall_tau_raw_average: 0.74\n      kendall_tau_mfrm_only: 0.77\n      kendall_tau_sequential: 0.76\n      kendall_tau_cyclicjudge: 0.78\n      kendall_tau_cyclicjudge_discard: 0.77\n      kendall_tau_dcr: 0.75\n      kendall_tau_uda: 0.79\n      kendall_tau_prefleak_calib: 0.76\n      kendall_tau_gmfrm: 0.77\n      best_baseline: \"UDA\"\n      delta_tau_vs_best_baseline: -0.02\n      delta_tau_vs_mfrm_only: 0.00\n      wilcoxon_p_gmfrm_vs_uda: 0.34\n      wilcoxon_p_gmfrm_vs_cyclicjudge: 0.62\n      spearman_rho_gmfrm: 0.89\n      spearman_rho_best_baseline: 0.91\n      rank_change_models_geq2_positions: 2\n      top5_stability_gmfrm: 0.80\n      top5_stability_best_baseline: 0.80\n      post_hoc_power_for_observed_delta: 0.12\n      n_models: 22\n    result_rationale: \"Under worst tier, Δτ is in [-0.02, 0.01] range. G-MFRM (τ=0.77) is actually slightly BELOW UDA (τ=0.79) — UDA's neural K-factor adjustment captures judge-specific biases that are the dominant source of ranking noise when contamination is negligible. CyclicJudge (τ=0.78) also edges out G-MFRM because round-robin bias elimination is effective when contamination is near-zero. The Wilcoxon test is massively underpowered (power=0.12 at N=22 models for detecting Δτ=0.02) — we cannot distinguish methods statistically. G-MFRM ties MFRM-only (both τ=0.77), confirming the contamination correction adds nothing when there's nothing to correct. Rank changes are minimal: only 2 models shift ≥2 positions between G-MFRM and best baseline, and these are within posterior uncertainty. This is the fatal result for Claim 3.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Main Comparison Table\"\n        columns: [\"Method\", \"Kendall_tau\", \"Spearman_rho\", \"Rank_changes_geq2\", \"Top5_stable\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Raw multi-judge average\", 0.74, 0.86, \"ref\", \"ref\"]\n          - [\"MFRM-only (Stage 1)\", 0.77, 0.89, 3, \"4/5\"]\n          - [\"Sequential (MFRM→contamination)\", 0.76, 0.88, 2, \"4/5\"]\n          - [\"CyclicJudge\", 0.78, 0.90, 3, \"4/5\"]\n          - [\"CyclicJudge + item discard\", 0.77, 0.89, 2, \"4/5\"]\n          - [\"DCR Factor\", 0.75, 0.87, 2, \"4/5\"]\n          - [\"UDA\", 0.79, 0.91, 4, \"4/5\"]\n          - [\"Preference Leakage calibration\", 0.76, 0.88, 2, \"4/5\"]\n          - [\"G-MFRM (ours)\", 0.77, 0.89, 2, \"4/5\"]\n      - name: \"Statistical Tests\"\n        columns: [\"Comparison\", \"Delta_tau\", \"Wilcoxon_p\", \"Power_at_alpha_0.05\", \"Significant?\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"G-MFRM vs UDA\", -0.02, 0.34, 0.12, \"No\"]\n          - [\"G-MFRM vs CyclicJudge\", -0.01, 0.62, 0.08, \"No\"]\n          - [\"G-MFRM vs MFRM-only\", 0.00, 0.91, 0.05, \"No\"]\n          - [\"G-MFRM vs Sequential\", 0.01, 0.55, 0.09, \"No\"]\n          - [\"G-MFRM vs Raw average\", 0.03, 0.18, 0.22, \"No\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Bootstrap confidence interval plot: all methods' τ CIs overlap heavily; G-MFRM CI centered at 0.77 with [0.71, 0.83] — indistinguishable from 5 other methods\"\n      - \"Rank change alluvial diagram: nearly static rankings across methods; 2-3 models shift 1-2 positions, driven by judge bias correction not contamination\"\n    analysis: \"Claim 3 is dead. G-MFRM does not improve over best baselines — it slightly underperforms UDA. Post-hoc power analysis (power=0.12) confirms the study is fundamentally underpowered to detect the observed effect size with N=22 models. Even with 50 models, power would be ~0.25 for Δτ=0.02. The ranking improvement is non-significant by any standard. MFRM-only and G-MFRM produce identical τ, confirming the contamination gating mechanism adds no signal. Pivot narrative: G-MFRM 'degrades gracefully' to MFRM level when contamination is absent — this is a feature, not a failure.\"\n\n  - name: \"contamination_magnitude_calibration\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-005\"\n    description: \"Estimate real contamination magnitude; determine if c_n matches mvp_001 (weak) or mvp_002 (strong)\"\n    expected_results:\n      real_cn_median: 0.15\n      real_cn_mean: 0.22\n      real_cn_max: 0.61\n      real_cn_iqr: [0.04, 0.32]\n      pct_models_cn_above_0.5: 9.1\n      pct_models_cn_above_1.0: 0.0\n      closest_mvp_match: \"below mvp_001\"\n      mvp_001_cn_mean_was: 0.5\n      ratio_real_to_mvp001: 0.44\n      constat_delta_correlation_with_cn: 0.32\n      constat_delta_median: 0.08\n      cn_distribution_shape: \"heavily right-skewed, mode near 0\"\n      bimodality_coefficient: 0.31\n    result_rationale: \"Under worst tier, real c_n median is in [0.05, 0.3] — we get 0.15, which is BELOW even mvp_001's assumption of c~0.5. The distribution is not bimodal but heavily right-skewed with mode near zero: most models have negligible contamination, 2-3 have moderate (0.4-0.6), none reach 1.0. This is well below the regime where G-MFRM's gating mechanism has meaningful leverage. The ConStat per-model delta validates only weakly (r=0.32) because both signals are near-floor — correlation is attenuated by floor effects and measurement noise. The finding itself IS informative: current benchmarks (MT-Bench 2024 refresh, Arena-Hard v2) appear to have effectively low contamination. This challenges assumptions in contamination literature that training data overlap produces large performance inflation on these specific benchmarks.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Contamination Magnitude Comparison\"\n        columns: [\"Source\", \"cn_median\", \"cn_mean\", \"cn_max\", \"Interpretation\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"MVP synthetic (mvp_001)\", 0.50, 0.50, 1.00, \"Assumed moderate\"]\n          - [\"MVP synthetic (mvp_002/003)\", 1.50, 1.50, 2.50, \"Assumed strong\"]\n          - [\"Real data (this experiment)\", 0.15, 0.22, 0.61, \"Negligible to weak\"]\n      - name: \"Per-Model c_n Estimates\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"c_n_posterior_mean\", \"95CI\", \"ConStat_delta\", \"Category\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"GPT-5.5\", 0.08, \"[0.01, 0.29]\", 0.05, \"negligible\"]\n          - [\"Claude Opus 4.7\", 0.05, \"[0.00, 0.21]\", 0.03, \"negligible\"]\n          - [\"Llama 4 405B\", 0.35, \"[0.06, 0.72]\", 0.15, \"weak\"]\n          - [\"Qwen3-235B\", 0.43, \"[0.08, 0.89]\", 0.18, \"weak-moderate\"]\n          - [\"DeepSeek-V4\", 0.61, \"[0.18, 1.12]\", 0.22, \"moderate (max)\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"c_n distribution: kernel density showing heavy pile-up near 0, thin tail to 0.6; overlaid with mvp_001 and mvp_002 assumed distributions for visual comparison of how much weaker real signal is\"\n      - \"ConStat delta vs G-MFRM c_n scatter: weak positive trend (r=0.32), large scatter, most points clustered near origin\"\n    analysis: \"This is the definitive worst-tier signal. Real contamination is far below synthetic assumptions. The mvp_002/003 success (43.8% RMSE reduction) required c_n~1.5 — real data has c_n~0.15 (10× weaker). Even mvp_001 assumed c~0.5. The G-MFRM's gating mechanism is solving a problem that barely exists on these benchmarks. Pivot implications: (1) the null finding about contamination magnitude is itself publishable — benchmarks may be cleaner than assumed; (2) G-MFRM's value is as a diagnostic tool that CONFIRMS low contamination rather than correcting for high contamination; (3) need to find or construct a benchmark with known high contamination for validation case.\"\n\n  - name: \"judge_count_sensitivity\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-006\"\n    description: \"Vary judges (2,4,6,8); measure G-MFRM degradation; leave-one-judge-out residual diagnostic\"\n    expected_results:\n      tau_8_judges: 0.77\n      tau_6_judges: 0.76\n      tau_4_judges: 0.75\n      tau_2_judges: 0.73\n      tau_mfrm_only_8_judges: 0.77\n      tau_mfrm_only_4_judges: 0.75\n      delta_gmfrm_vs_mfrm_8j: 0.00\n      delta_gmfrm_vs_mfrm_6j: 0.00\n      delta_gmfrm_vs_mfrm_4j: 0.00\n      delta_gmfrm_vs_mfrm_2j: -0.01\n      cn_identifiability_threshold_judges: \"not reached\"\n      leave_one_out_max_residual_change: 0.04\n      leave_one_out_same_family_bias_detected: false\n      convergence_2j_rhat_max: 1.25\n      convergence_4j_rhat_max: 1.15\n      convergence_6j_rhat_max: 1.10\n    result_rationale: \"Under worst tier, G-MFRM never outperforms MFRM-only at any judge count because contamination is negligible — the gating mechanism adds parameters without adding signal. At 2 judges, G-MFRM actually slightly underperforms MFRM-only (Δτ=-0.01) due to over-parameterization with insufficient data. Convergence degrades progressively as judges decrease (R̂=1.25 at 2 judges) because c_n parameters are completely unidentifiable with sparse judge coverage and weak e_i. The leave-one-judge-out diagnostic shows no systematic same-family bias pattern — residual changes are small (max 0.04) because contamination effects are too weak to create detectable judge-model interactions. The practical conclusion is that with ≥4 judges, G-MFRM is harmless (degrades gracefully to MFRM level) but never beneficial for ranking.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Judge Count Sensitivity\"\n        columns: [\"N_judges\", \"tau_GMFRM\", \"tau_MFRM\", \"Delta\", \"R_hat_max\", \"ESS_min_cn\"]\n        rows:\n          - [8, 0.77, 0.77, 0.00, 1.12, 58]\n          - [6, 0.76, 0.76, 0.00, 1.10, 65]\n          - [4, 0.75, 0.75, 0.00, 1.15, 42]\n          - [2, 0.73, 0.74, -0.01, 1.25, 22]\n      - name: \"Leave-One-Judge-Out Residual Diagnostic\"\n        columns: [\"Removed_judge\", \"Family_overlap?\", \"Max_residual_change\", \"Systematic_bias?\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"GPT-5.5\", \"Yes (GPT family targets)\", 0.04, \"No\"]\n          - [\"Claude Opus 4.7\", \"Yes (Claude targets)\", 0.03, \"No\"]\n          - [\"DeepSeek-V4-Pro\", \"Yes (DeepSeek targets)\", 0.02, \"No\"]\n          - [\"Qwen3-235B-A22B\", \"Yes (Qwen targets)\", 0.03, \"No\"]\n          - [\"Llama 4 Maverick\", \"Yes (Llama targets)\", 0.02, \"No\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Performance degradation curve: two nearly overlapping lines (G-MFRM and MFRM-only) both declining gently from 8→2 judges; gap is zero or negative throughout\"\n      - \"R̂ vs judge count: exponential increase below 4 judges for c_n parameters specifically, while θ and d parameters remain well-behaved\"\n    analysis: \"This experiment confirms G-MFRM adds nothing over MFRM-only at any judge count in the worst tier. The contamination facet is unidentifiable in practice. However, the graceful degradation finding is publishable as a safety property: G-MFRM doesn't HARM rankings when contamination is absent. The leave-one-judge-out diagnostic is useful as a methodological contribution regardless of contamination magnitude — it provides a principled way to detect preference leakage without requiring contamination to be present.\"\n\n  - name: \"exposure_indicator_ablation\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-007\"\n    description: \"Ablation: contribution of each exposure source to e_i quality; leave-one-out analysis\"\n    expected_results:\n      full_pipeline_alpha: 0.24\n      leave_out_performance_gap_alpha: 0.15\n      leave_out_temporal_alpha: 0.22\n      leave_out_logprober_alpha: 0.20\n      performance_gap_unique_contribution: 0.09\n      temporal_unique_contribution: 0.02\n      logprober_unique_contribution: 0.04\n      performance_gap_only_tau: 0.76\n      temporal_only_tau: 0.75\n      logprober_only_tau: 0.75\n      full_pipeline_tau: 0.77\n      no_ei_mfrm_only_tau: 0.77\n      delta_any_source_vs_no_ei: [-0.02, 0.01]\n    result_rationale: \"Under worst tier, e_i pipeline has internal consistency α=0.24 (floor). When we ablate sources, the full pipeline (α=0.24) is already so weak that removing any single source barely changes downstream τ. The performance gap is the strongest single source but still weak (unique contribution = 0.09 to α). Temporal and LogProber add negligible incremental signal. Critically, ALL configurations of e_i produce the same downstream τ (0.75–0.77) because the contamination correction itself is inconsequential — varying e_i quality doesn't matter when c_n ≈ 0. The 'no e_i' condition (MFRM-only, τ=0.77) matches or beats all e_i conditions, confirming the entire exposure estimation pipeline is effort without payoff on these benchmarks.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Exposure Source Ablation\"\n        columns: [\"Configuration\", \"Alpha\", \"e_i_mean\", \"e_i_SD\", \"Downstream_tau\", \"Delta_vs_MFRM_only\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Full pipeline (3 sources)\", 0.24, 0.18, 0.22, 0.77, 0.00]\n          - [\"Without performance gap\", 0.15, 0.16, 0.20, 0.76, -0.01]\n          - [\"Without temporal\", 0.22, 0.17, 0.21, 0.77, 0.00]\n          - [\"Without LogProber\", 0.20, 0.19, 0.23, 0.76, -0.01]\n          - [\"Performance gap only\", \"N/A\", 0.15, 0.19, 0.76, -0.01]\n          - [\"No e_i (MFRM-only)\", \"N/A\", \"N/A\", \"N/A\", 0.77, \"ref\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Bar chart of unique variance contribution per source: all bars near zero, performance gap slightly taller but still below meaningful threshold\"\n      - \"Downstream τ forest plot: all configurations' CIs overlap completely, including MFRM-only baseline\"\n    analysis: \"The ablation confirms that no e_i source meaningfully contributes to ranking quality under worst tier. This is not a pipeline failure per se — it's that the signal being measured (item exposure) is genuinely near-zero on these benchmarks. Pivot narrative: the pipeline is VALIDATED for measuring exposure (internal structure exists, α=0.24 > 0), but the underlying construct is at floor. This supports the 'benchmarks are clean' narrative contribution.\"\n\n  - name: \"facet_fit_diagnostics\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-008\"\n    description: \"Measurement quality: infit/outfit, person-fit, divergent transitions, posterior predictive checks\"\n    expected_results:\n      theta_infit_mean: 1.02\n      theta_outfit_mean: 1.05\n      theta_misfit_pct: 4.5\n      b_infit_mean: 0.99\n      b_outfit_mean: 1.03\n      b_misfit_pct: 6.2\n      d_infit_mean: 1.01\n      d_outfit_mean: 1.04\n      d_misfit_pct: 12.5\n      cn_infit_mean: 1.15\n      cn_outfit_mean: 1.28\n      cn_misfit_pct: 36.4\n      divergent_transitions_total: 142\n      divergent_transitions_pct: 2.2\n      posterior_predictive_p_global: 0.41\n      posterior_predictive_p_items_below_0.05_pct: 8.3\n      lz_stage1_vs_cn_stage3_correlation: 0.18\n      stage1_emri_flagged_models: 3\n      cn_flagged_same_models: 1\n    result_rationale: \"Under worst tier, θ, b, and d facets fit well because MFRM structure is appropriate for multi-judge data (these are just standard psychometric facets). But c_n shows substantial misfit: 36.4% of models have outfit > 1.3, indicating the rank-1 contamination model (c_n·e_i) doesn't describe the residual patterns. This is because when true contamination is near-zero, the c_n parameters are fitting noise rather than signal — any nonzero c_n estimate absorbs heterogeneous residual variance, producing outfit. The lz (person-fit) from Stage 1 correlates only weakly (r=0.18) with c_n from Stage 3, meaning the two stages are identifying different 'aberrant' patterns — further evidence that c_n is not measuring a coherent construct on this data. Divergent transitions (2.2%) cluster around c_n parameters near the half-Cauchy boundary. The model fits well OVERALL (global posterior predictive p=0.41) because c_n terms are near-zero and the model is effectively MFRM.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Facet Fit Summary\"\n        columns: [\"Facet\", \"N\", \"Infit_mean\", \"Outfit_mean\", \"Misfit_pct\", \"Interpretation\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"theta (ability)\", 22, 1.02, 1.05, 4.5, \"Good fit\"]\n          - [\"b (difficulty)\", 580, 0.99, 1.03, 6.2, \"Good fit\"]\n          - [\"d (severity)\", 8, 1.01, 1.04, 12.5, \"Acceptable (1/8 judges borderline)\"]\n          - [\"c_n (contamination)\", 22, 1.15, 1.28, 36.4, \"Poor fit — construct at floor\"]\n      - name: \"Convergence Diagnostics Detail\"\n        columns: [\"Parameter_group\", \"R_hat_median\", \"R_hat_max\", \"ESS_median\", \"ESS_min\", \"Divergent_pct\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"theta\", 1.01, 1.03, 892, 412, 0.1]\n          - [\"b\", 1.00, 1.02, 1105, 523, 0.2]\n          - [\"d\", 1.01, 1.02, 945, 678, 0.1]\n          - [\"c_n\", 1.05, 1.18, 156, 34, 1.8]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Wright map (person-item) from Stage 1: well-calibrated, items span ability range adequately\"\n      - \"c_n outfit vs c_n posterior mean: negative trend — models with higher c_n have WORSE fit, suggesting noise-fitting\"\n      - \"Trace plots for 3 worst-mixing c_n parameters: visible stuck chains, slow drift\"\n    analysis: \"Diagnostics reveal a split personality: G-MFRM as MFRM (θ,b,d) works well; the contamination extension (c_n) is poorly identified and misfitting. This is consistent with worst tier where c_n attempts to model a near-absent signal. The 36.4% c_n misfit rate would normally indicate model failure, but reframed: it shows the contamination term is unnecessary for this data. Pivot value: these diagnostics themselves demonstrate G-MFRM's utility as a diagnostic framework — the misfit detection tells practitioners 'contamination is not a concern on this benchmark,' which is actionable information.\"\n\n  - name: \"confounding_formal_observation\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-009\"\n    description: \"Formal OVB bias derivation + Monte Carlo validation across realistic parameter ranges; synthetic c_n recovery\"\n    expected_results:\n      bias_formula_derived: true\n      monte_carlo_n_conditions: 48\n      monte_carlo_reps_per_condition: 100\n      bias_magnitude_at_cn_0.15: 0.02\n      bias_magnitude_at_cn_0.5: 0.08\n      bias_magnitude_at_cn_1.5: 0.31\n      bias_direction_prediction_accuracy: 0.94\n      cn_recovery_rmse_at_real_magnitude: 0.42\n      cn_recovery_95ci_coverage_at_real: 0.78\n      cn_recovery_rmse_at_mvp_magnitude: 0.18\n      cn_recovery_95ci_coverage_at_mvp: 0.91\n      confounding_scatter_r_at_real_cn: 0.12\n      confounding_scatter_r_at_mvp_cn: 0.65\n      orthogonality_test_real_data: 0.09\n    result_rationale: \"The formal OVB observation is valid regardless of contamination magnitude — it's a mathematical identity. Monte Carlo confirms: bias direction prediction is 94% accurate across all conditions. BUT the practical bias magnitude scales linearly with c_n. At real-data contamination levels (c_n≈0.15), the sequential estimator bias is only 0.02 logits — negligible relative to posterior uncertainty. The confounding scatter r drops from 0.65 (mvp_002/003 regime) to 0.12 (real-data regime) because d_j and c_n·e_i are approximately orthogonal when c_n is near-zero. Synthetic c_n recovery shows the method WORKS at high contamination (RMSE=0.18, coverage=0.91 at mvp levels) but FAILS at real-data levels (RMSE=0.42, coverage=0.78) — the posterior is too wide to be informative. This confirms the core assumption: 'd_j and c_n·e_i approximately orthogonal in this dataset' — confounding exists in theory but not in practice.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Monte Carlo Bias Magnitude by c_n Level\"\n        columns: [\"c_n_regime\", \"Bias_theta_RMSE\", \"Scatter_r\", \"Sequential_degradation_pct\", \"Practical_significance\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"c_n = 0.15 (real)\", 0.02, 0.12, 1.8, \"Negligible\"]\n          - [\"c_n = 0.50 (mvp_001)\", 0.08, 0.38, 12.5, \"Marginal\"]\n          - [\"c_n = 1.00\", 0.19, 0.52, 28.3, \"Moderate\"]\n          - [\"c_n = 1.50 (mvp_002/003)\", 0.31, 0.65, 43.8, \"Large\"]\n      - name: \"Synthetic c_n Recovery at Real-Data Magnitude\"\n        columns: [\"Metric\", \"c_n=0.15\", \"c_n=0.50\", \"c_n=1.50\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"c_n RMSE\", 0.42, 0.28, 0.18]\n          - [\"95% CI coverage\", 0.78, 0.87, 0.91]\n          - [\"c_n correlation (true vs fitted)\", 0.35, 0.72, 0.92]\n          - [\"Pct models correctly ranked by c_n\", 0.55, 0.78, 0.94]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Bias magnitude surface plot: x=c_n level, y=rho(d,c·e), z=theta bias — steep only in high-c_n, high-rho corner\"\n      - \"Confounding scatter at 4 c_n levels: panel plot showing scatter tightening to diagonal as c_n decreases\"\n    analysis: \"The formal observation remains a valid theoretical contribution — OVB exists regardless of magnitude. But the practical punchline under worst tier: the bias is negligible (0.02 logits) because contamination is too low to create meaningful confounding. The synthetic c_n recovery at real-data magnitude (correlation 0.35) confirms G-MFRM cannot reliably rank models by contamination susceptibility when the signal is this weak. Pivot framing: 'we derive the confounding mechanism and show it activates under conditions X; current benchmarks happen to NOT meet these conditions; the correction is available when needed.'\"\n\n  - name: \"wildbench_replication\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-010\"\n    description: \"Replicate G-MFRM pipeline on WildBench as third benchmark for cross-benchmark generalization\"\n    expected_results:\n      wildbench_n_items: 1024\n      wildbench_n_judges: 6\n      wildbench_kendall_tau_gmfrm: 0.74\n      wildbench_kendall_tau_mfrm: 0.75\n      wildbench_kendall_tau_uda: 0.76\n      wildbench_delta_tau_gmfrm_vs_best: -0.02\n      wildbench_cn_median: 0.09\n      wildbench_cn_max: 0.38\n      wildbench_ei_median: 0.08\n      wildbench_convergence_rhat_max: 1.14\n      wildbench_convergence_ess_min: 41\n      cross_benchmark_cn_correlation: 0.28\n      cross_benchmark_theta_correlation: 0.92\n    result_rationale: \"WildBench uses more diverse, harder items sourced from real user interactions — these items are LESS likely to be contaminated than MT-Bench/Arena-Hard because they're more recent and less standardized. Under worst tier, WildBench shows even lower contamination (median c_n=0.09) than the other benchmarks. G-MFRM slightly underperforms both MFRM-only and UDA because the contamination parameters add noise. Cross-benchmark c_n correlation is weak (r=0.28) — models don't show consistent contamination patterns across benchmarks, suggesting what little contamination exists is benchmark-specific (item-level memorization) rather than model-level training data overlap. Cross-benchmark θ correlation (0.92) confirms ability estimates are stable regardless of contamination correction method.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Cross-Benchmark Comparison\"\n        columns: [\"Benchmark\", \"tau_GMFRM\", \"tau_MFRM\", \"tau_UDA\", \"cn_median\", \"ei_median\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"MT-Bench\", 0.76, 0.75, 0.78, 0.17, 0.14]\n          - [\"Arena-Hard\", 0.78, 0.77, 0.79, 0.14, 0.11]\n          - [\"WildBench\", 0.74, 0.75, 0.76, 0.09, 0.08]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cross-benchmark c_n scatter matrix: 3 panels showing weak correlations between benchmarks — contamination is benchmark-specific, not model-intrinsic\"\n    analysis: \"WildBench replication confirms worst-tier pattern generalizes: negligible contamination, no ranking benefit from G-MFRM. The low cross-benchmark c_n correlation (0.28) suggests that contamination — what little exists — is item-specific rather than reflecting stable model-level susceptibility. This weakens the c_n construct validity further but supports the 'benchmarks are clean' narrative.\"\n\n  - name: \"adversarial_robustness\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-011\"\n    description: \"Test G-MFRM robustness against adversarial contamination following Wang et al. (ICLR 2026)\"\n    expected_results:\n      adversarial_injection_levels: [0.0, 0.3, 0.5, 1.0, 1.5]\n      tau_gmfrm_at_0.0: 0.77\n      tau_gmfrm_at_0.3: 0.76\n      tau_gmfrm_at_0.5: 0.78\n      tau_gmfrm_at_1.0: 0.82\n      tau_gmfrm_at_1.5: 0.87\n      tau_sequential_at_1.5: 0.79\n      tau_mfrm_at_1.5: 0.75\n      crossover_point_cn: 0.7\n      cn_recovery_at_injection_1.5: 0.81\n      detection_power_at_real_cn: 0.15\n      detection_power_at_injected_1.5: 0.92\n    result_rationale: \"Under worst tier, natural contamination is negligible — but we can INJECT synthetic contamination to demonstrate G-MFRM works when contamination IS present. By artificially inflating scores for specific models on high-e_i items, we create a controlled setting. At injection level 1.5 (matching mvp_002/003), G-MFRM recovers well (τ=0.87 vs sequential's 0.79). The crossover point where G-MFRM starts outperforming is c_n≈0.7 — above real-data levels but demonstrating the method's potential. Detection power at real c_n levels is only 0.15 (cannot detect what isn't there), but jumps to 0.92 at injected 1.5. This experiment redeems the method theoretically while confirming it's unnecessary on current benchmarks.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Adversarial Injection Results\"\n        columns: [\"Injection_level\", \"tau_GMFRM\", \"tau_Sequential\", \"tau_MFRM\", \"cn_recovery_corr\", \"Detection_power\"]\n        rows:\n          - [0.0, 0.77, 0.76, 0.77, 0.35, 0.15]\n          - [0.3, 0.76, 0.76, 0.76, 0.42, 0.25]\n          - [0.5, 0.78, 0.77, 0.76, 0.58, 0.45]\n          - [1.0, 0.82, 0.79, 0.75, 0.74, 0.78]\n          - [1.5, 0.87, 0.79, 0.75, 0.81, 0.92]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Crossover plot: G-MFRM vs baselines as function of injected contamination level — lines cross around c_n=0.7, with G-MFRM pulling ahead in high-contamination regime\"\n      - \"Annotated vertical line at 'real data level' (c_n≈0.15) showing all methods equivalent there\"\n    analysis: \"Adversarial robustness provides the strongest pivot evidence: G-MFRM works when contamination exists, it just doesn't exist on current benchmarks. The crossover at c_n=0.7 gives a principled threshold for when G-MFRM becomes necessary. Framing: 'G-MFRM is an insurance policy — unnecessary today but protective against future contamination increases as training data grows.' Also validates the methodology for historical benchmarks (e.g., MMLU original) where contamination IS known to be high.\"\n```","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"025abe8b-2d2c-421e-a4ec-955a56bb312b","total_cost_usd":0.432938,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":25617,"output_tokens":12908,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":12908,"cache_read_input_tokens":25617,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":8747,"outputTokens":22,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.008857,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000},"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":12908,"cacheReadInputTokens":25617,"cacheCreationInputTokens":14170,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.424081,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"7f4c1ebd-fa51-4dab-9642-056cea2a6587"}
